 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "kerry_valentia",
   "title": "The Valentia Cable",
   "text": "Out west, Valentia Island was the European end of the transatlantic telegraph. The first cable, in 1858, worked for only a few weeks. In 1866 the giant steamship Great Eastern laid one that lasted, to Heart's Content in Newfoundland, and news that took about ten days by ship took minutes. For the first time, the two sides of the Atlantic could talk on the same day."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "MUCKROSS HOUSE. A Victorian mansion of 1843, built for the Herberts and tidied up for a visit by Queen Victoria in 1861. Some say the visit nearly bankrupted them. It was only tea."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "ROSS CASTLE. A tower house on the shore of Lough Leane, 15th century and built by the O'Donoghue clan. The legend says the chieftain still sleeps under the lake, and rises every seven years."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "KILLARNEY NATIONAL PARK. Ireland's first national park, 1932. Home of the last native herd of red deer in the country. They don't read the sign, either."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "TORC WATERFALL. About twenty metres of white water tumbling down an old oak wood. Torc means 'wild boar' in Irish. We've no idea why the boar needs a waterfall."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "LADIES' VIEW. Named for Queen Victoria's ladies-in-waiting, who were so taken with the view in 1861 that the spot was named for them. The view hasn't charged for it since."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "MACGILLYCUDDY'S REEKS. The highest mountains in Ireland, with Carrauntoohil at 1,038 metres. The name is an English mangle of an old clan. 'Reek' means a rocky peak, not a smell."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 4,
   "plaque": "ring_of_kerry_skellig",
   "title": "Skellig Michael",
   "text": "About twelve kilometres off the Kerry coast, early Christian monks built beehive huts of dry stone on the peak of Skellig Michael, reached by hundreds of steep stone steps. It became a World Heritage Site in 1996. Puffins nest there now, and are very good at steps."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "ring_of_kerry_puck_fair",
   "title": "Puck Fair",
   "text": "Every August in Killorglin, a wild mountain goat is caught and crowned King Puck, and sits in state above the town for three days of the Puck Fair, one of Ireland's oldest fairs. Then he is taken back to the mountains, none the worse and a lot more famous."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "INNISFALLEN. The island out in Lough Leane, with its abbey, where monks wrote the Annals of Innisfallen. The channel from this shore is too deep to walk; Brook-wade would see you across, and it comes with the Urban Stone from Keeper Ray in Liverpool."
  }
 ],
